1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,830
Hello, this is AR Solar System: an Android
AR app for museums.

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:09,590
Visitors scan printed planet cards, make a
guess, and watch the planets prove it.

3
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:15,830
The project is designed for a table in the
planet gallery of an astronomy museum,

4
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:20,630
where visitors scan printed cards with a
tablet the museum provides.

5
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:25,370
Today, labels show numbers like 3.7 m/s².

6
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:28,210
Visitors can read them, but they can't
test them.

7
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:32,410
Our goal is to answer three questions: why
you jump higher on Mars,

8
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:36,270
what sets a day and a year, and how
different the planets are in size.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:38,130
AR cards suit this space.

10
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:42,730
The comparison is physical and shared, the
Sun card is a light you can move,

11
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:45,390
and the museum only needs to print cards.

12
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,270
Because visitors stand and stay briefly,
we use short prompts,

13
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,730
no sign-in, and a screen that stays awake.

14
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,910
Our visitor is a child on a family visit,
sharing one phone.

15
00:00:55,960 --> 00:01:00,120
The journey has six stages, from arriving
to the reveal,

16
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:02,970
and excitement peaks when the answer
appears.

17
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,650
Every interaction starts from a card or a
fingertip:

18
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:10,610
tap, drag, pinch, add a second card, or
add the Sun card.

19
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:13,610
We also tested the guess-first loop on
paper,

20
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:17,850
as a Wizard of Oz test, with a person
acting as the app.

21
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,190
Here is the prototype in action.

22
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:27,820
The visitor opens the app and taps scan.

23
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:33,710
Pointing the camera at the Earth card
brings the planet onto the table.

24
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:41,700
A pinch resizes it, and a drag spins it,
so visitors can look from any side.

25
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,790
Now the Sun card joins.

26
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:54,710
As the visitor moves it, Earth's lit half
turns to face it, showing day and night.

27
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,390
Adding a second card, Saturn,

28
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:06,765
opens the lab with four experiments: jump,
day, year, and size.

29
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,440
First: who jumps higher?

30
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:12,470
The visitor guesses Earth, and taps jump.

31
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,120
Saturn wins, by 4 centimetres!

32
00:02:18,170 --> 00:02:20,670
Its gravity is close to Earth's.

33
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:23,770
Next: whose year is shorter? Tap orbit,

34
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,750
and both planets circle the Sun card.

35
00:02:26,973 --> 00:02:29,890
Earth laps Saturn again and again.

36
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,030
The core prototype was built between the
10th and the 14th of November,

37
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:39,890
including Sun-card lighting, four
experiments, and an Android build.

38
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:44,490
Next, we will print the cards and test
with real visitors under gallery lighting,

39
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:47,910
where glare and tracking loss are the main
risks.

40
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:55,365
These are our sources for the learning
approach, the planetary data, and the
tools.

41
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:58,250
Thank you. Scan, guess, and watch.
